A **service execution platform for hotels** is software that makes a property's standards run on every shift, and proves that they did. It turns SOPs into timed tasks on staff phones, captures photo and supervisor evidence as the work happens, and compounds that evidence into an audit-ready service record.

It is a young category, so it is often confused with older ones. This article explains what it is, what it is not, and how to tell whether your hotel needs one. For a short definition, see our [glossary entry](/glossary/service-execution-platform).

## Why a new category was needed

Hotels already buy plenty of software for standards. There are document systems that store SOPs, learning management systems that teach them, checklist apps that list them and chat groups that broadcast them. And yet the most common problem we hear from general managers is the same: *the standard is written, but I cannot tell whether it ran today.*

That gap exists because none of those tools sits at the moment of work. They sit before it (learning), beside it (documents), or after it (reports). A service execution platform sits inside it.

## The four moves of service execution

Every service execution platform does four things in order. In Mise we call them the loop:

1. **Standard.** The SOP is written once as clear steps with a target time, reference photos and evidence requirements.
2. **Timed task.** The standard arrives on the right person's phone as a task with a countdown, at the moment the work happens.
3. **Evidence.** Steps are timestamped as they close; the steps that matter need a photo before they close; a supervisor signs off in the task.
4. **Service record.** Every completed task writes an attributable entry: which standard, which version, who, where, when, with what proof.

If a product does not do all four, it is doing something else.

## Service execution platform vs LMS

This is the comparison we are asked about most, because both involve standards and staff.

| | LMS | Service execution platform |
|---|---|---|
| Unit | Course or module | Timed task |
| When it is used | Before or between shifts | During the shift |
| What it records | Enrolment, completion, scores | Steps, times, photos, sign-off |
| The question it answers | Did the learner finish the course? | Did the standard run in room 208 at 08:39? |
| Audit output | Completion certificates | Service record of executed standards |

An LMS is the right tool when the job is to teach: induction content, formal certification, compliance courses. It is the wrong tool for proving that the standard was followed on Tuesday morning, because it was never designed to be present on Tuesday morning.

That is why Mise is not an LMS. It does not manage courses or learners. It manages the work. For the detailed comparison, see [how Mise compares with a hotel LMS](/compare/mise-vs-hotel-lms).

## Service execution platform vs checklist app

Checklist apps are closer to the work. They put a list on a phone. But a tick carries no evidence, no target time and no version of the standard, and it can be added at the end of the shift without the work behind it. The difference is proof. A service execution platform gates the key steps on photos and records sign-off inside the task. See [SOP software vs checklist apps](/compare/sop-software-vs-checklist-app).

## Service execution platform vs document management

A document system answers "where is the latest version of the SOP?" That is useful, and a service execution platform answers it too, because every standard is versioned. But storing a standard does not execute it. A hotel with a perfect document library can still have a floor full of [ghost SOPs](/problems/ghost-sop).

## What a service execution platform for hotels must get right

The category only works if staff actually use it on shift, which sets a high bar:

- **It must be built for the phone in one hand.** A 340px screen, one thumb, bright daylight, a budget Android phone, mobile data.
- **It must need no integration to start.** If a pilot waits for a PMS integration, it never starts.
- **Managers need a different interface.** A live picture of the floor on a desktop, with exceptions first.
- **Standards owners need a focused workspace.** Writing and publishing a standard should take minutes.
- **Evidence must be captured, not uploaded.** Proof taken inside the task at the moment of work is credible; proof uploaded later is a claim.

That is why Mise is built as three separate interfaces, not one responsive compromise. The [platform page](/platform) shows each one.

## A shift on a service execution platform

It helps to see the category in motion. Here is a morning on the Mise demo property, Aurora Grand Colombo (all data fictional):

| Time | What happens | What is recorded |
|---|---|---|
| 07:00 | Maya opens the staff app; her first timed task is on screen | Shift start |
| 07:52 | Room 204 reset begins; steps and reference photos on screen | Task start, standard version |
| 08:14 | Room 206 at step 3 of 8, on time against its 26-minute target | Step timestamps |
| 08:39 | Room 208 vanity photo taken; the gated step closes | Photo evidence, step 4 |
| 08:42 | Elena reviews the evidence on the manager dashboard and signs off | Supervisor sign-off |
| 08:47 | Room 210 released to the front office | Release |
| 09:15 | Room 305 flagged for maintenance and blocked | Exception surfaced to managers |
| 15:25 | Handover sent after its checklist; Jonas acknowledges | Handover and acknowledgement |

Nobody filled in a report. Nobody walked every corridor to check. By the end of the shift, the property holds a complete, attributable record of which standards ran, where, when and with what evidence. That record is what an LMS, a checklist app or a document system cannot produce, because none of them is present at 08:39.

The same record serves different people. The operations manager sees exceptions first. The HR director sees who acknowledged the current version of each standard. The general manager sees consistency across floors. The auditor, when they come, sees a filterable history instead of a folder assembled the night before.

## How to tell whether your hotel needs one

Ask yourself five questions:

1. If someone asks whether room 208 was reset to standard this morning, can you prove it without a phone call?
2. Do your supervisors spend most of their shift walking to check work?
3. Does audit preparation involve rebuilding evidence?
4. When an experienced attendant leaves, does service quality dip?
5. Do two staff members describe the same standard differently?

If you answered "no" to the first question or "yes" to any of the others, you have an execution gap, not a documentation gap. Those are the [six hotel operations problems](/problems) a service execution platform exists to close.

## What it is not

A service execution platform is not a PMS: it does not handle reservations, rates or folios. It is not an HR system or payroll. It is not a messaging app. It works alongside all of them, covering the one part of the operation none of them can see: whether the standard ran.
